WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.737
Database design comes before trustworthy analysis.

00:00:03.737 --> 00:00:11.092
If the structure is unclear, even a syntactically correct query can produce confusing or unreliable results.

00:00:11.092 --> 00:00:17.162
That is why we begin by deciding what we are modeling and what one row in each table should mean.

00:00:17.162 --> 00:00:20.652
A practical design process starts with scope.

00:00:20.652 --> 00:00:23.652
What real-world problem are we representing?

00:00:23.652 --> 00:00:28.072
Then we identify the main entities, connect them through relationships,

00:00:28.072 --> 00:00:30.757
and define rules that must always hold.

00:00:30.757 --> 00:00:34.557
Normalization helps us reduce unnecessary repetition,

00:00:34.557 --> 00:00:38.927
while constraints help prevent invalid data from entering the database.

00:00:38.927 --> 00:00:44.792
A useful final question is this: if a different application inserts data tomorrow,

00:00:44.792 --> 00:00:48.232
will the database itself still protect the important rules?

00:00:48.232 --> 00:00:52.337
Good design should not depend only on one user remembering what to do.

00:00:52.337 --> 00:01:00.602
Keep a real project in mind as we work through this section—for example, an online store separating customers,

00:01:00.602 --> 00:01:02.977
orders, products, and order lines.

00:01:02.977 --> 00:01:08.212
The technical feature matters because it changes what questions we can answer reliably and how easily another

00:01:08.212 --> 00:01:11.812
analyst can understand our work.

00:01:11.812 --> 00:01:18.717
As you listen, try to connect each SQL or data concept to a decision you might need to defend later:

00:01:18.717 --> 00:01:21.802
what the data means, what one row represents, what can be missing,

00:01:21.802 --> 00:01:25.903
and what assumptions the transformation introduces.
